{
  "step_1": "The decision reason evaluates skewness to determine preference. Skewness refers to the distribution of probabilities across outcomes. A lottery is left-skewed if more probability is assigned to favorable outcomes (higher payouts) relative to unfavorable ones (zero). In this case, both lotteries have a non-zero outcome (2500 or 2400 Euros) and a zero outcome. The key is to compare the probability mass on the positive outcome versus the zero outcome. Lottery A has a 33% chance of 2500 Euros and 67% chance of 0. Lottery B has a 34% chance of 2400 Euros and 66% chance of 0. Thus, A has 33% on the favorable outcome, B has 34%. Since 34% > 33%, Lottery B has a slightly higher probability on the favorable outcome. Therefore, B is more left-skewed than A. The reason prefers the more left-skewed lottery, which would be B. Thus, the decision reason is applicable and can be evaluated using the given probabilities and outcomes.",
  "step_2": "The individual states that the probability of winning is almost identical between the two lotteries, and therefore they would choose the one with the higher prize amount (2500 Euros vs. 2400 Euros). The individual does not mention probabilities of the outcomes, nor do they discuss skewness, distribution, or the shape of the probability function. The justification is based solely on the magnitude of the prize, not on the probability distribution or skewness. The deliberation process focuses on the prize amount as the deciding factor, not on the distribution of probabilities across outcomes.",
  "step_3": "The information relevant to the decision reason — the skewness of the lotteries — is not considered by the individual. The individual does not mention probability distribution, skewness, or any comparison of how the probabilities are allocated between the positive and zero outcomes. Instead, the individual bases the decision on the higher prize amount, ignoring the slight difference in probabilities. The decision reason requires evaluating the skewness (i.e., whether more probability is on the favorable outcome), which was not part of the reported deliberation. Therefore, the individual did not use the skewness-based decision reason. The justification does not reflect the rule of preferring the more left-skewed lottery. Hence, the decision reason was not used.",
  "final_assessment_score": 0
}

{
  "step_1": "The decision reason evaluates skewness to determine preference. Skewness refers to the distribution of probabilities across outcomes. A lottery is left-skewed if a greater portion of probability is assigned to favorable outcomes (higher values) compared to unfavorable ones (lower values). In this case, Lottery B has a 80% chance of 4000 Euros and a 20% chance of 0 Euros. This means 80% of the probability mass is on the higher outcome (4000), and 20% on the lower outcome (0). This indicates a right-skewed distribution (more probability on the higher end), not left-skewed. A left-skewed lottery would have more probability on the lower outcome (e.g., more chance of 0 than 4000). Since Lottery A offers 3000 Euros with 100% probability, it is symmetric and has no skewness. Therefore, neither lottery is left-skewed. The decision reason, which prefers the more left-skewed lottery, does not apply to either lottery. Thus, the reason cannot be applied to this decision problem.",
  "step_2": "The individual considers the high probability (80%) of winning 4000 Euros in Lottery B, but concludes that the difference in outcomes is not large enough to justify taking the risk. The individual chooses Lottery A (3000 Euros for sure) because of the perceived small difference between the guaranteed amount and the potential gain in Lottery B. The justification focuses on the risk aversion and the subjective perception of the difference in outcomes, not on any measure of skewness. There is no mention of probability distribution, skewness, or any comparison of the shape of the outcome distribution. The deliberation centers on risk and the perceived magnitude of the difference in gains, not on left-skewness or distributional asymmetry.",
  "step_3": "The information relevant to the decision reason (skewness) is not present in the lotteries as described — Lottery B is actually right-skewed, not left-skewed, and Lottery A is symmetric. Therefore, the condition for applying the decision reason (preference for a more left-skewed lottery) is not met. Moreover, the individual’s justification does not mention skewness, distribution shape, or any reasoning based on asymmetry of outcomes. Instead, the reasoning is based on perceived difference in outcomes and risk aversion. Since neither the relevant information nor the justification aligns with the decision reason, the individual did not use the skewness-based reason. Thus, the decision reason was not used.",
  "final_assessment_score": 0
}

