  "step_3": "The decision reason requires evaluating skewness — specifically, whether a greater portion of probability is assigned to favorable outcomes (zero or positive) compared to unfavorable (negative) outcomes. In this case, both lotteries have nearly all their probability mass on zero (99.9% and 99.8%), so both are highly right-skewed, not left-skewed. Thus, neither lottery satisfies the condition for being left-skewed, and the reason cannot be applied. The individual’s reasoning is based on choosing the lottery with the smaller loss amount, which is a different decision criterion (risk aversion or loss minimization), not skewness. There is no mention or application of skewness in the verbal report. Therefore, the decision reason was not used. The individual did not consider the distributional shape or skewness of the lotteries, nor did they apply the rule that prefers more left-skewed lotteries. Hence, the reason was not used.",
